---
/**
 * Integrations — "Plugged in, read only".
 *
 * DESIGN: IN-C "Chip grid", Figma `wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU`, frame `152:2` on
 * page `Section — Integrations` (`150:2`). OWNER DECISION, 2026-08-02, and it
 * SUPERSEDES the earlier build of this file.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous version implemented IN-A, "The boundary
 * line": six inbound arrows, one lit rule, a READ ONLY capsule sitting on it,
 * and a struck return arrow hanging off its right-hand end. The recommendation
 * frame `157:2` had said "Ship A. Fall back to C." The owner has reviewed the
 * variants and taken C. Build it; do not re-argue it, and do not reintroduce
 * IN-A's parts one at a time — a chip grid with a struck arrow bolted onto it
 * is neither variant.
 *
 * WHAT IN-C IS, in its own words on `152:2`: "the legacy 3x3, rebuilt to the
 * craft standard: gradient border, 5% wash, specular top edge, two-colour glow.
 * Closest to what already ships and the lowest-risk to build. It states
 * read-only in words rather than drawing it, which is its one weakness."
 *
 * ══ THE ONE WEAKNESS, AND HOW IT IS COVERED ═══════════════════════════════
 *
 * That last sentence is the whole editorial problem with this variant and it is
 * worth being explicit about, because it is the thing that can quietly rot.
 * IN-A carried the read-only claim as GEOMETRY: arrows in, one struck arrow
 * out. A reader could not miss it and could not misread it. A chip grid carries
 * no direction at all, so the claim has to be carried in language, and language
 * is easier to soften later than a drawing is. Three things carry it here and
 * all three are load-bearing:
 *
 *   1. THE EYEBROW. "Plugged in, read only", above the heading.
 *   2. THE SCOPE ON EVERY CHIP. Each source prints the actual scope it is
 *      connected with — `Sites.Read.All`, `Files.Read.All`, "Sign-in only" —
 *      not the words "read only". That is STRONGER than the Figma frame, which
 *      prints "read only" nine times as a caption. A scope is checkable; a
 *      caption is an assertion. The scope line is the reason this section can
 *      claim read-only at all, and it may not be replaced with a friendlier
 *      phrase.
 *   3. THE CLAIM AND THE ASIDE beneath the grid, in two sentences that must not
 *      be merged. See below.
 *
 * ══ THE READ-ONLY CLAIM, AND WHAT WAS CORRECTED ═══════════════════════════
 *
 * CARRIED OVER UNCHANGED FROM THE IN-A BUILD, because it is accuracy rather
 * than layout and the variant switch does not touch it.
 *
 * The legacy line reads: "Read-only connectors. We never write back to your
 * files or change a setting." It sits under a 3x3 grid naming Microsoft 365,
 * Entra ID SSO, Google Workspace, Okta, Slack, Teams, Google Drive, Zapier and
 * Make — which is exactly the nine chips frame `152:2` draws, each labelled
 * "read only". Checked against the platform repo, that is FALSE for three of
 * the nine and UNSUPPORTED for two more:
 *
 *   Slack          crowagent-platform/web/lib/connectors/providers.ts:103
 *                  scopes ["chat:write", "chat:write.public", "channels:read"]
 *                  slack.ts posts via chat.postMessage. It writes.
 *   Teams          providers.ts:138, scope "ChannelMessage.Send".
 *                  ms-teams.ts sends into a channel. It writes.
 *   Google Drive   providers.ts, scope "drive.file", commented in that file as
 *                  "access ONLY to files this app creates". Narrow, but it is
 *                  still a write: the connector's whole job is export.
 *   Zapier, Make   No connector exists. CONNECTOR_PROVIDER_CONFIG holds exactly
 *                  three providers and neither is one of them. The only mention
 *                  anywhere is WorkflowBuilder.tsx:517, describing them as a
 *                  BRIDGE used to reach Slack and Teams. Naming them as
 *                  connected on the homepage claims an integration that is not
 *                  built.
 *
 * OWNER DECISION: NARROW THE LIST. So this section names only what the repo
 * evidences as read-only. The three write connectors are named in the aside
 * with their real function, because saying nothing about Slack and Teams after
 * the live site named them would read as a quiet deletion rather than a
 * correction. The two that do not exist are simply gone.
 *
 * THE GRID IS SIX CHIPS, NOT NINE, AND THAT IS THE CORRECTION MADE VISIBLE.
 * `152:2` draws nine. Building nine would put "read only" under Slack, Teams,
 * Google Drive, Zapier and Make on the homepage, which is the defect this
 * section exists to fix. Six chips in a 3x2 grid is the same composition at the
 * only length the evidence supports.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT FOR CORRECTNESS. Every name, every scope, the claim and the
 * aside are in the HTML and painted at first render. `data-lit` only decides
 * when a decorative light is allowed to run along the chips' top edges.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Source {
  /** As a security reviewer would name it. */
  name: string;
  /**
   * The evidence, in the form it appears on the page: the actual scope, or the
   * mechanism. NOT marketing copy, and NOT the words "read only". This is the
   * row that makes the claim checkable, and with IN-A's struck arrow gone it is
   * now the strongest per-source signal on the section. See the header note.
   */
  scope: string;
  /** A vector mark from the existing brand set, or undefined for a plain dot. */
  logo?: string;
}

/**
 * SIX SOURCES, EVERY ONE EVIDENCED FROM THE PLATFORM REPO.
 *
 *   SharePoint      api/app/services/content_sources.py:953
 *                   scopes=("Sites.Read.All",)
 *   OneDrive        content_sources.py:1006
 *                   scopes=("Files.Read.All",)
 *   Confluence      content_sources.py:1060, an API token that inherits the
 *                   issuing user's own permissions. No scope is escalated.
 *   Entra ID SSO    web/lib/auth/sso-enforcement.ts + sso_provider_configs.
 *   Okta            The same SAML path. Both are identity providers: the
 *   Google Workspace  assertion says who is signing in and which organisation
 *                   they belong to, and nothing is written back to the IdP.
 *
 * ANYTHING ADDED TO THIS ARRAY NEEDS THE SAME KIND OF LINE. If a connector's
 * read-only scope cannot be pointed at in the repo, it does not belong in this
 * grid, and the honest move is to leave it off rather than to soften the claim
 * around it.
 */
const SOURCES: Source[] = [
  {
    name: 'SharePoint',
    scope: 'Sites.Read.All',
    logo: '/Assets/brand/integrations/color-microsoft.svg',
  },
  {
    name: 'OneDrive',
    scope: 'Files.Read.All',
    logo: '/Assets/brand/integrations/color-microsoft.svg',
  },
  {
    name: 'Confluence',
    scope: 'Your own permissions',
  },
  {
    name: 'Entra ID SSO',
    scope: 'Sign-in only',
    logo: '/Assets/brand/integrations/color-microsoft-entra-id.svg',
  },
  {
    name: 'Okta',
    scope: 'Sign-in only',
    logo: '/Assets/brand/integrations/color-okta.svg',
  },
  {
    name: 'Google Workspace',
    scope: 'Sign-in only',
    logo: '/Assets/brand/integrations/color-google.svg',
  },
];
---

{/* `sv-floor`, on the <section> so it runs the full viewport width. The
    reasoning trace above is base and the closing card below sits on the lit
    step, so the ladder still alternates. See styles/surfaces.css. */}
<Section
  eyebrow="Plugged in, read only"
  title="Your evidence already exists. We read it where it lives."
  id="integrations"
  class="sv-floor"
>
  {/*
    `data-lit` at threshold 0.35. The grid is complete before it means anything:
    every chip, every name and every scope is drawn at full strength. This
    attribute only decides when a light is allowed to run along the top edges
    that are already there.
  */}
  <div class="in" data-spot="off" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.35">
    <div class="in__amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="in__amb-a"></b>
      <b class="in__amb-b"></b>
      {/* The cursor light and the grain, inside the wrapper so both stay behind
          every word and inherit its mask. <i>, so neither joins the drift. */}
      <i class="sv-spot"></i>
      <i class="sv-grain"></i>
    </div>

    {/*
      A LIST OF NAMES WITH THEIR SCOPES, NOT A LOGO WALL. Each source is a real
      text node with its real scope beside it, so the section reads correctly
      with every pixel of the chip treatment removed. That was true of the IN-A
      build too and it matters more here, because the scope line is now the
      section's per-source evidence rather than a caption beside a drawing.
    */}
    <ul class="in__grid">
      {
        SOURCES.map((s) => (
          <li class="in__chip">
            {/*
              THE SPECULAR, AND IT IS ON THE SAFEST SURFACE IN THE SECTION. A
              1px highlight travelling the chip's TOP EDGE only. Nothing sits on
              that edge and nothing reads across it, so light there costs no
              contrast anywhere. Sweeping the chip's FILL would put light under
              the name and the scope, which the motion rules forbid outright.
              Clipped by the chip's own overflow, so it can never widen the page
              while it travels — the failure that is invisible to any check
              measuring a settled layout.
            */}
            <span class="in__spec" aria-hidden="true" />

            {s.logo ? (
              <img
                class="in__logo"
                src={s.logo}
                alt=""
                aria-hidden="true"
                width="18"
                height="18"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
            ) : (
              /* No vector mark exists for this one in the brand set. A neutral
                 token-coloured dot holds the slot rather than an invented or
                 borrowed logo, which would be a fabricated brand asset. */
              <span class="in__logo in__logo--dot" aria-hidden="true" />
            )}
            <span class="in__name">{s.name}</span>
            <span class="in__scope">{s.scope}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    {/*
      THE CLAIM, NARROWED TO WHAT THE REPO EVIDENCES. Two sentences, and the
      split between them is the whole correction. The first is a guarantee about
      the six sources drawn above. The second names the three connectors that DO
      write, with their real function, so nothing on this page implies a
      read-only guarantee over a surface that posts messages or creates files.

      DO NOT MERGE THESE INTO ONE SENTENCE and do not restore the legacy blanket
      wording. With IN-A's struck arrow gone this pair is doing more work than it
      was: it is now the only place the read-only boundary is stated as a
      boundary rather than as a scope value. See the header note.
    */}
    <p class="in__claim">
      Every source above is read only. We do not write to your files, change a
      setting in your tenant, or create an account.
    </p>
    <p class="in__aside">
      Slack, Teams and Google Drive are separate outbound connectors, used to send
      a notification or export a file you have asked for. They are not sources we
      read from.
    </p>

    <p class="in__more">
      <a href="/security">How we handle your data</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .in {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* ── Ambient, per the lighting rig (Figma `141:2` L03) ───────────────────*/
  /* Bleed exactly one gutter, never a percentage. The containing block is the
     section body, which is the measure inset by --gutter, so this reaches the
     viewport edge at every width below the measure and stops there. A
     percentage bleed overflowed the page at 390 and 768.

     `data-field` opts the two fields into the shared ambient layer declared in
     styles/tokens.css: the always-on --m-drift, and the one-beat m-arrive
     entrance every section on this page shares. */
  .in__amb {
    position: absolute;
    /* Scroll-driven parallax throw. See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 24px;
    inset-block: -18%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* All four edges are masked. `overflow: hidden` stops the bleed widening
       the page but cuts a 150px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in
       a straight line draws a visible lighter rectangle. One radial mask fades
       every edge at once, so there is no axis for it to miss. See the full note
       in MarketShape.astro, including the mistake of masking only one axis.  */
    /*
     * `closest-side`, NOT `115% 105%`, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CORRECTION.
     *
     * The earlier value was chosen to fade all four edges at once, which it
     * did — but only partly. An ellipse of 115% x 105% has semi-axes of 0.575w
     * and 0.525h, so the middle of the left edge sits at r = 0.5 / 0.575 =
     * 0.87 of the way along a ramp that only reaches transparent at 1.0. About
     * 25% of the field survives there. On a 150px blur whose own falloff is
     * nearly finished, 25% of nearly nothing is nothing, which is why it held.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 raises the orb stops by roughly 1.5x to
     * 1.9x, and 25% of that is a visible vertical line. Measured on the built
     * result at 1440: a lighter RECTANGLE across this section and the four
     * others sharing this recipe, one gutter in from each side — exactly the
     * defect the original note describes and thought it had closed.
     *
     * `closest-side` gives semi-axes of 0.5w and 0.5h, so the ramp reaches
     * transparent EXACTLY at the middle of every edge and past it at every
     * corner. There is no arithmetic left to get wrong, and it stays correct
     * when a section changes shape.
     */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      rgb(0 0 0) 42%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .in__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .in__amb-a {
    width: 560px;
    height: 400px;
    left: -6%;
    top: -26%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .in__amb-b {
    width: 520px;
    height: 400px;
    right: -6%;
    bottom: -28%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.38;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .in__amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 280px; }
  }

  /* ── The chip grid ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     THREE COLUMNS, TWO ROWS. `152:2` draws a 3x3; six evidenced sources fill
     3x2, which is the same composition at the only length the evidence
     supports. See the header note on why it is not nine. */
  .in__grid {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(12px, 1.4vw, 18px);
  }

  /*
   * A CHIP, NOT A SMALL CARD, AND THE DISTINCTION STILL DECIDES WHETHER IT
   * TAKES `surface`. It does not, for the same reason as before: a card is a
   * destination and this is a label with a value beside it — no heading, no
   * action, nothing to click. `surface` would bring --radius-card, --blk-pad, a
   * backdrop filter and a hover lift, and would make six labels look like six
   * things you can open. What IN-C asks for is four properties, and every one
   * of them comes from a token:
   *
   *   gradient border    the double-background trick below
   *   5% wash            a --c-teal color-mix over --c-card
   *   specular top edge  --gloss-inset, the same one-token highlight the cards
   *                      and now the buttons take
   *   two-colour glow    teal core, violet halo, the ramp's two ends
   *
   * THE GRADIENT BORDER IS TWO BACKGROUNDS, NOT A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The fill is
   * painted to the padding box and the ramp to the border box, with the border
   * itself transparent, so the ramp shows only in the 1px the border occupies.
   * A ::before overlay would need its own stacking and would sit over the
   * travelling specular; this needs neither and costs one declaration.
   */
  .in__chip {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 18px minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: auto auto;
    column-gap: 10px;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 14px 16px;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    background:
      linear-gradient(
          160deg,
          color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 5%, var(--c-card)),
          var(--c-card) 78%
        )
        padding-box,
      linear-gradient(
          135deg,
          color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 48%, transparent),
          color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 34%, transparent)
        )
        border-box;
    /* The specular top-light plus a two-colour glow: a tight teal core under a
       wide violet halo. One colour reads as a blur; two read as light, and both
       are points on the ramp this page already runs. */
    box-shadow:
      var(--gloss-inset),
      0 0 18px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 12%, transparent),
      0 14px 40px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet-deep) 22%, transparent);
    /* Clips the travelling specular to the chip's own top edge. */
    overflow: hidden;
    transition:
      background-image var(--dur) var(--ease),
      box-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  /* ══ THE CHIP ANSWERS A CURSOR ════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Six chips, ZERO :hover rules — one of the four sections
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6 names, and the most obviously dead of them,
   * because a grid of six tiles is the shape a reader most expects to respond.
   *
   * WHAT A CHIP DOES: its wash steps up from --c-card to the fourth surface
   * value, its gradient border opens towards both ends of the ramp, its
   * two-colour glow roughly doubles, its mark comes to full opacity and its
   * scope line comes up from --c-text-muted to --c-text-sub.
   *
   * IT DOES NOT LIFT, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING TO PRESERVE IF THIS IS EVER
   * EDITED. A chip is a label with a value beside it: no heading, no action,
   * nothing to click. styles/surfaces.css restricts the card lift to elements
   * that actually navigate precisely so a page cannot advertise six affordances
   * that are not there, and this section's header note already refuses
   * `surface` for the same reason. Light, never movement.
   *
   * BOTH TEXT ENDPOINTS CLEAR THE FLOOR: --c-text on --c-lit is about 19:1 and
   * --c-text-sub about 12:1, so every intermediate frame of the transition
   * clears it too, which is what the axe gate samples for.
   */
  .in__logo,
  .in__name,
  .in__scope {
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease), opacity var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .in__logo { opacity: 0.86; }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .in__chip:hover {
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(160deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 7%, var(--c-lit)), var(--c-raised) 78%),
        linear-gradient(
          135deg,
          color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 78%, transparent),
          color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 62%, transparent)
        );
      box-shadow:
        inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16),
        0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 22%, transparent),
        0 18px 48px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet-deep) 38%, transparent);
    }

    .in__chip:hover .in__logo { opacity: 1; }
    .in__chip:hover .in__scope { color: var(--c-text-sub); }
    .in__more a:hover { color: var(--c-text); }
  }

  .in__logo {
    grid-row: 1 / 3;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  /*
   * THE PLACEHOLDER KEEPS THE FULL 18px SLOT, and that is the fix rather than a
   * detail. It used to shrink the box to 8px and re-centre it with a 5px inline
   * margin. Geometrically that is centred, and it was: measured, the dot sat on
   * exactly the same axis as every logo. It still read as misaligned, because a
   * row of six icons is judged on optical mass, not on box centres, and one
   * icon carrying less than a fifth of the area of its neighbours breaks the
   * row however well it is positioned.
   *
   * So the slot is identical for every chip and the dot is painted inside it.
   * A gradient rather than a child element because there is no child to style:
   * the placeholder is an empty aria-hidden span, and adding one would put a
   * second node in the markup for a decorative mark.
   *
   * Full opacity, unlike the vector marks above it. This dot is --c-teal, and
   * teal on this site means verified; a marker held at 86% is a marker somebody
   * has dimmed. The logos are third-party artwork and take the quieter rest
   * state instead.
   */
  .in__logo--dot {
    opacity: 1;
    background: radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, var(--c-teal) 0 4px, transparent 4.5px);
    /* drop-shadow follows the painted shape; box-shadow would ring the 18px
       box and give the placeholder a halo the logos do not have. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 6px var(--c-teal-glow));
  }

  .in__name {
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* The scope. Mono, because it is a machine value quoted verbatim rather than
     a description of one, and it is this section's per-source evidence now that
     the struck arrow has gone with IN-A. --c-text-muted measures 9.88:1 on
     --c-bg. */
  .in__scope {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── The claim ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/
  .in__claim {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.8;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .in__aside {
    margin: var(--stack-1) auto 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.8;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .in__more {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .in__more a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-cyan);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .in__more a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ══ THE LIGHT ALONG THE CHIP EDGES ═══════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.35.
   * CADENCE one beat of travel per chip, a sixth of a beat apart, on the
   * shared --m-cycle. The sixth chip lands at two beats.
   * PLAYS: LOOPS, then eighteen beats of rest.
   * REDUCED MOTION nothing travels and nothing is missing.
   *
   * THE FIGURE CHANGED WITH THE VARIANT; THE CADENCE DID NOT. IN-A ran a light
   * down six inbound arrows at one beat each, a sixth of a beat apart. IN-C has
   * no arrows, so the same light runs along six chip TOP EDGES on exactly the
   * same clock. That is the point of expressing cadence in stations rather than
   * in milliseconds: the section's drawing was replaced wholesale and its place
   * in the page's rhythm did not move. See the motion grammar in
   * styles/tokens.css.
   *
   * WHITE CORE, TEAL BLOOM. A lamp, not a marker (`141:2` L04), and the same
   * band the hero rail, the spine, the both-sides beams and the closing card's
   * sweep all use.
   *
   * A NOTE ON WHAT IS NO LONGER HERE. The IN-A build carried a standing rule
   * that the struck return arrow must never animate, because a refusal is a
   * fact and not a performance. IN-C has no struck arrow to hold still, so that
   * rule now lives entirely on the reasoning trace and the both-sides rail,
   * where the refusal markers still fire once and never loop. Nothing in this
   * section is a refusal any more; the read-only boundary is carried in words
   * and in six printed scopes, and neither of those moves at all.
   */
  .in__spec {
    /* No animation and no trigger: zero pixels. */
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .in__spec {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      /* The chip's own top edge, inside its radius. */
      top: 0;
      inset-inline: 0;
      height: 1px;
      z-index: 1;
      background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent 34%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 70%, transparent) 44%,
        var(--c-text) 50%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 70%, transparent) 56%,
        transparent 66%
      );
      opacity: 0;
    }

    [data-lit='on'] .in__spec {
      animation: in-spec var(--m-cycle) var(--m-ease-travel) infinite;
    }

    /* A sixth of a beat apart, in reading order, so the six chips fill exactly
       one beat between the first starting and the last starting. A delay on an
       infinite animation is a permanent phase offset, so the stagger survives
       every repeat rather than only the first. */
